  <main class="legal">
    <h1>Scanner</h1>
    <p class="updated mono">Automated infrastructure discovery for your homelab</p>

    <p>The StdOut Scanner is a lightweight Go binary that discovers your infrastructure and pushes the results to your StdOut account. It runs as a Docker container alongside your other services — no agent installation, no privileged access by default.</p>

    <h2>What the scanner discovers</h2>

    <p>The scanner is modular. You choose what to enable based on what you want to know about your infrastructure.</p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Module</th><th>What it finds</th><th>Default</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>docker</code></td><td>Containers, images, ports, networks, compose projects, health status, restart policies, volume mounts, environment variable names (never values)</td><td>On</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>metrics</code></td><td>CPU %, memory usage/limit, network I/O, disk I/O, PID count per container. Host-level CPU, memory, load average, and uptime.</td><td>On</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>network</code></td><td>All devices on your subnet — IPs, hostnames, MAC addresses, vendor identification (80+ manufacturers), open ports, device type classification (server, NAS, router, printer, IoT, etc.)</td><td>Off</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dns</code></td><td>Reverse DNS, mDNS/Bonjour service advertisements, reverse proxy detection (nginx, Traefik, Caddy, HAProxy, Envoy), TLS certificate inspection with expiry dates and SANs</td><td>Off</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>auth</code></td><td>Which services require login, what type of auth they use (basic, bearer, form-based), and SSO provider identification (Authentik, Keycloak, Okta, Auth0, Azure AD, Google, GitHub, Authelia, Cloudflare Access, and more)</td><td>Off</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What the scanner does NOT do</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Never reads secrets.</strong> Environment variable names only — never values. No config file contents. No passwords, API keys, or tokens.</li>
      <li><strong>Never attempts authentication.</strong> Auth detection checks response headers (401/403) and login page patterns. It never submits credentials, brute forces, or tests passwords.</li>
      <li><strong>Never captures traffic.</strong> Network scanning uses ICMP ping and TCP connect probes. No packet sniffing, no deep packet inspection, no man-in-the-middle.</li>
      <li><strong>Never phones home.</strong> The scanner only talks to your StdOut instance (the URL you configure). No telemetry, no analytics, no external calls.</li>
      <li><strong>Never writes to your infrastructure.</strong> All access is read-only. The Docker socket is mounted read-only (<code>:ro</code>). No containers are started, stopped, or modified.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Getting the scanner</h2>

    <p>The scanner is distributed as a Docker image. Pull it from GitHub Container Registry:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>docker pull charlieseay/stdout-scanner:latest</code></pre>

    <p>Or run it directly — Docker will pull the image automatically:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>docker run --rm \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  charlieseay/stdout-scanner version</code></pre>

    <h2>Quick start</h2>

    <h3>1. Create an API token</h3>

    <p>Go to <a href="/app/settings">Settings → API Tokens</a> and generate a new token. Copy it — it's shown only once.</p>

    <h3>2. Run the interactive setup</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>docker run -it --rm \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  -v ~/.stdout:/data \
  charlieseay/stdout-scanner init</code></pre>

    <p>The setup interview walks you through:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Detecting what's accessible (Docker socket, network interfaces)</li>
      <li>Choosing which modules to enable</li>
      <li>Configuring your StdOut URL and API token</li>
      <li>Setting up a scan schedule (optional)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Your configuration is saved to <code>~/.stdout/scanner.yaml</code> (or <code>/data/scanner.yaml</code> inside Docker).</p>

    <h3>3. Run your first scan</h3>

    <pre is:raw><code>docker run --rm \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  -v ~/.stdout:/data \
  charlieseay/stdout-scanner scan</code></pre>

    <p>The scanner discovers your infrastructure and pushes the results to StdOut. Go to your dashboard to review and confirm the import.</p>

    <h2>Scan modes</h2>

    <h3>One-shot scan</h3>

    <p>The default. Scans once and exits. Good for manual runs or cron jobs.</p>

    <pre is:raw><code># Scan and push to StdOut
docker run --rm \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  -v ~/.stdout:/data \
  charlieseay/stdout-scanner scan

# Scan and print markdown locally (no push)
docker run --rm \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  charlieseay/stdout-scanner scan --output markdown

# Scan and print JSON
docker run --rm \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  charlieseay/stdout-scanner scan --output json</code></pre>

    <h3>Delta scan</h3>

    <p>Compares the current scan against the last one and reports only changes. The scanner saves state to <code>/data/last-scan.json</code>.</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>docker run --rm \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  -v ~/.stdout:/data \
  charlieseay/stdout-scanner scan --delta</code></pre>

    <p>Delta scans report:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>New containers, removed containers, image upgrades</li>
      <li>Status or health changes (container went unhealthy)</li>
      <li>New or disappeared network devices</li>
      <li>Port changes on network devices</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If nothing changed, nothing is pushed — no noise in your dashboard.</p>

    <h3>Scheduled scan</h3>

    <p>Run the scanner as a persistent container that scans on a schedule:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>docker run -d --name stdout-scanner \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  -v ~/.stdout:/data \
  charlieseay/stdout-scanner scan \
  --schedule daily --delta \
  --token YOUR_TOKEN --url https://your-stdout-instance.com</code></pre>

    <p>Schedule options: <code>hourly</code>, <code>daily</code>, <code>daily@03:00</code>, <code>weekly@sun@03:00</code>.</p>

    <p>The scanner also polls your StdOut account settings for schedule changes — so you can adjust the frequency, time, and enabled modules from the <a href="/app/settings">Settings</a> page without restarting the container.</p>

    <h2>Network scanning</h2>

    <p>Network discovery is optional and off by default. To enable it, the scanner needs access to your LAN:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code># Option 1: Host networking (full LAN visibility)
docker run --rm --net=host \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  -v ~/.stdout:/data \
  charlieseay/stdout-scanner scan \
  --scan-network --subnets 192.168.0.0/24

# Option 2: Specify in config (set during init)
# subnets are saved in scanner.yaml</code></pre>

    <p>Network scanning uses ICMP ping and TCP connect probes (not raw packet capture). It also queries SNMP (v2c, community "public") for device metadata like model names and uptime.</p>

    <h3>Privacy considerations</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>The scanner only scans subnets you explicitly configure</li>
      <li>By default, it scans only the Docker host's primary subnet</li>
      <li>It never captures traffic content — only connection metadata (IP, port open/closed)</li>
      <li>SNMP queries use the read-only "public" community string</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Full module scan</h2>

    <p>Enable everything at once with <code>--full</code>:</p>

    <pre is:raw><code>docker run --rm --net=host \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro \
  -v ~/.stdout:/data \
  charlieseay/stdout-scanner scan --full \
  --subnets 192.168.0.0/24</code></pre>

    <h2>CLI reference</h2>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Command</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>init</code></td><td>Interactive setup — detects environment, configures modules and token</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>scan</code></td><td>Run a scan (default if no command specified)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>version</code></td><td>Print version and exit</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Scan flags</h3>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Flag</th><th>Description</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--token</code></td><td>StdOut API token (overrides config)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--url</code></td><td>StdOut instance URL (overrides config)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--output json|markdown</code></td><td>Print results locally instead of pushing to StdOut</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--config /path/to/config.yaml</code></td><td>Config file path (default: auto-detected)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--full</code></td><td>Enable all discovery modules</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--scan-network</code></td><td>Enable network device discovery</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--subnets 192.168.0.0/24</code></td><td>Subnet(s) to scan (comma-separated)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--scan-dns</code></td><td>Enable DNS and service discovery</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--scan-auth</code></td><td>Enable auth detection</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--scan-snmp</code></td><td>Enable SNMP queries (default: true)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--delta</code></td><td>Compare against previous scan, report changes only</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--state-file /path</code></td><td>State file for delta comparison (default: /data/last-scan.json)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--schedule daily</code></td><td>Run on schedule: hourly, daily, daily@HH:MM, weekly@day@HH:MM</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--skip-host</code></td><td>Skip host info collection</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--skip-metrics</code></td><td>Skip resource metrics</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--dry-run</code></td><td>Scan but don't push to StdOut (prints JSON)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Self-hosted scanner</h2>

    <p>The scanner is open source and works with both SaaS and self-hosted StdOut instances. Point it at any StdOut URL — there's nothing SaaS-specific about the protocol.</p>

    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

    <h3>Scanner can't connect to Docker</h3>
    <p>Make sure the Docker socket is mounted: <code>-v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock:ro</code></p>

    <h3>Network scan finds no devices</h3>
    <p>Inside Docker, the scanner sees the container's virtual network, not your LAN. Use <code>--net=host</code> to give it host networking, or run the scanner binary directly on the host.</p>

    <h3>SNMP returns no data</h3>
    <p>Most devices respond to SNMP v2c with community string "public", but some have SNMP disabled or use a different community. This is informational only — the scanner works fine without SNMP.</p>

    <h3>Push fails with 401</h3>
    <p>Your API token may be revoked or invalid. Generate a new one in <a href="/app/settings">Settings → API Tokens</a>.</p>

    <h3>Delta shows the scanner container itself as added/removed</h3>
    <p>This is expected when running the scanner as a one-shot <code>docker run --rm</code> container. The scanner container appears in one scan and disappears in the next. Use a persistent container with <code>--schedule</code> to avoid this.</p>
  </main>

